  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Single entry records one side only, so there is no trial balance to prove the books.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The statement derives capital as the excess of what is owned over what is owed.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    600,000 − 220,000 = ₦380,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    470,000 + 50,000 − 20,000 − 380,000 = ₦120,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    They are the owner's withdrawal, not a cost of earning the profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    New money from the owner is not profit, so it must be removed from the movement in capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    25 ÷ 125 = 20 per cent of sales.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    20 ÷ 80 = 25 per cent of cost.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    240,000 × 1.25 = ₦300,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    400,000 × 0.25 = ₦100,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cost of sales = 400,000 × 0.75 = 300,000; closing stock = 50,000 + 260,000 − 300,000 = ₦10,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    420,000 + 5,000 + 110,000 − 80,000 = ₦455,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    300,000 + 4,000 + 75,000 − 60,000 = ₦319,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It works from a list of what is owned and owed, and the capital is the balancing figure.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Without double entry there is no trial balance to extract.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A count is the only direct evidence; the rest is inference.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The goods left the business for the owner, so drawings are debited and purchases reduced.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    200,000 + 80,000 − 30,000 = ₦250,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cost is 60 per cent of sales: 500,000 × 0.60 = ₦300,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The trading account is reconstructed, and the expenses are taken from the cash book afterwards.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    90,000 − 10,000 + 14,000 = ₦94,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    60,000 − 15,000 = ₦45,000, the rest being a prepayment.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    That is exactly what makes the records incomplete.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The excess of opening assets over opening liabilities is the opening capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Accruals, credit sales and capital movements all break the link between cash and profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A statement of affairs lists assets and liabilities at a date, not the flows of the year.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    80,000 × 1.20 = ₦96,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    16,000 ÷ 96,000 = 16.7 per cent, which is the margin equivalent of a 20 per cent mark-up.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    180,000 + 320,000 = ₦500,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A higher closing stock reduces cost of sales and raises the profit.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The owner has taken out more than the business earned.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cash received, discounts allowed and the movement in debtors together give the credit sales.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    It is added back when reconstructing credit sales, because the full sale was credited to the debtor.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The firm paid less cash than the goods cost, so the discount is added back to the cash paid.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One works from capital, the other from the trading account.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The profit comes from comparing it with the opening capital, after adjusting for drawings and new capital.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    An audit requirement would push the firm towards full double entry, not away from it.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    420,000 + 60,000 − 300,000 = ₦180,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cost is 75 per cent of sales: 600,000 × 0.75 = ₦450,000.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The trading account supplies the gross profit that the expense schedule then reduces to net profit.
